1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,107
Four questions a CTO can ask, calmly,
after a week in which someone rebased.

2
00:00:09,447 --> 00:00:12,674
"Asha pushed a commit to the
feature branch on Tuesday.

3
00:00:12,714 --> 00:00:17,545
On Wednesday it was gone from GitHub, and
after her next pull it was gone from her

4
00:00:17,585 --> 00:00:20,752
laptop as well. Nobody deleted
anything. Where is it?"

5
00:00:21,092 --> 00:00:25,445
"The pull request had three commits before
the review and three commits after it,

6
00:00:25,485 --> 00:00:28,808
with the same titles. The reviewer
approved the first version.

7
00:00:28,848 --> 00:00:31,248
What exactly is different
in the second one?"

8
00:00:31,588 --> 00:00:35,573
"Why does the history of the ingest
branch contain every commit twice?"

9
00:00:35,914 --> 00:00:40,508
"A developer says the rebase finished without
an error and one of his commits is not

10
00:00:40,548 --> 00:00:42,826
in the branch any more. Is that possible?"

11
00:00:43,166 --> 00:00:48,219
The fourth answer is "yes". Hold on to that
one. You'll see how it can happen before

12
00:00:48,259 --> 00:00:53,191
this video ends. And all four answers start
from one fact, which is the subject of

13
00:00:53,231 --> 00:00:53,858
this video.

14
00:00:55,698 --> 00:01:00,765
Welcome back to Git and GitHub Deep Mastery.
Pull up a chair: a new module starts

15
00:01:00,805 --> 00:01:06,060
here. And here's that fact, in the textbook's
words: a rebase doesn't move or change

16
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:09,339
commits. It writes new
commits and then moves a ref.

17
00:01:09,379 --> 00:01:14,193
A commit is one saved snapshot of the
project, and a ref is a name, such as a

18
00:01:14,233 --> 00:01:16,147
branch, that holds a commit ID.

19
00:01:16,487 --> 00:01:21,340
The old commits are still in the object
database. Other clones still have them.

20
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:24,932
The server still has them,
until someone pushes over them.

21
00:01:24,972 --> 00:01:30,073
Every surprise in the rebase module is a
consequence of two histories existing side

22
00:01:30,113 --> 00:01:35,090
by side for a while: the one you rewrote,
and the one everybody else still holds.

23
00:01:35,430 --> 00:01:38,747
So this first video does two
things and nothing more.

24
00:01:38,787 --> 00:01:44,067
It shows you a plain rebase, before and
after, and then rebuilds that rebase by hand

25
00:01:44,107 --> 00:01:48,185
from three simpler commands, so
that there's no magic left in it.

26
00:01:48,225 --> 00:01:53,379
And it opens an original commit and its
copy side by side, field by field, so that

27
00:01:53,419 --> 00:01:56,292
you can say exactly why
the copy has a new ID.

28
00:01:56,632 --> 00:02:01,391
The project in this module is ragkit, a
small retrieval-augmented answering service.

29
00:02:03,231 --> 00:02:04,643
After this video you can:

30
00:02:04,983 --> 00:02:09,416
Describe a rebase as copying commits
onto a new base and moving the branch.

31
00:02:09,756 --> 00:02:14,778
Explain from the commit object why each
copy has a new ID even with an identical

32
00:02:14,818 --> 00:02:15,094
diff.

33
00:02:15,434 --> 00:02:18,013
Show where the original
commits are after the rebase.

34
00:02:18,353 --> 00:02:23,399
Perform a rebase by hand with git switch
--detach, git cherry-pick and a branch

35
00:02:23,439 --> 00:02:23,721
move.

36
00:02:25,561 --> 00:02:30,365
In one sentence: git rebase <upstream> 🟡
CAUTION takes the commits that your branch

37
00:02:30,405 --> 00:02:35,268
has and <upstream> doesn't have, creates a
copy of each one on top of <upstream>, in

38
00:02:35,308 --> 00:02:38,304
order, and then points your
branch at the last copy.

39
00:02:38,643 --> 00:02:41,814
Why would you want that?
There are two everyday uses.

40
00:02:41,854 --> 00:02:46,843
One is bringing a feature branch up to date
before review, so that the reviewer and

41
00:02:46,883 --> 00:02:50,780
CI, the automated checks, see your
change on top of today's main.

42
00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:54,900
The other is tidying your own commits
before anyone else reads them.

43
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:59,626
Precisely. The manual describes git
rebase <upstream> as four steps.

44
00:02:59,966 --> 00:03:04,721
One. Make the list of commits to replay:
everything reachable from the current

45
00:03:04,761 --> 00:03:09,762
branch and not from <upstream>, the same
set that git log <upstream>..HEAD prints,

46
00:03:09,802 --> 00:03:14,496
minus commits whose change is already
in <upstream>, and minus merge commits.

47
00:03:14,536 --> 00:03:19,475
Both exceptions get their own videos. On
screen, the list is the three commits of

48
00:03:19,515 --> 00:03:20,213
feat/rerank.

49
00:03:20,553 --> 00:03:25,486
Two. Detach HEAD at <upstream>. Detached
means that HEAD names a commit directly,

50
00:03:25,526 --> 00:03:27,880
here the tip of main, and not a branch.

51
00:03:28,220 --> 00:03:31,152
Three. Replay the commits
one by one, oldest first.

52
00:03:31,192 --> 00:03:35,929
Each replay is a cherry-pick: one commit's
change, applied on top of HEAD as a new

53
00:03:35,969 --> 00:03:40,532
commit. Inside, it's a three-way merge
whose base is the parent of the original

54
00:03:40,572 --> 00:03:45,309
commit, whose "ours" side is HEAD, and
whose "theirs" side is the original commit.

55
00:03:45,349 --> 00:03:50,145
The result is committed with the original
author, author date and message, and with

56
00:03:50,185 --> 00:03:51,485
you, now, as committer.

57
00:03:51,825 --> 00:03:56,907
Four. Move the branch ref to the last new
commit, and attach HEAD to the branch

58
00:03:56,947 --> 00:03:57,296
again.

59
00:03:57,636 --> 00:04:02,534
Two words in that description deserve
attention. "Reachable": Git has no notion of a

60
00:04:02,574 --> 00:04:07,413
parent branch, so the set of commits is
computed from the graph, not from where you

61
00:04:07,453 --> 00:04:11,763
believe the branch started. And "copy":
a commit object is never modified.

62
00:04:11,803 --> 00:04:14,526
The copies are new objects
with new commit IDs.

63
00:04:14,866 --> 00:04:20,196
Inside .git: new commit objects, and new
tree objects wherever the combined content

64
00:04:20,236 --> 00:04:25,372
differs from anything stored before. Blobs,
the stored file contents, are reused

65
00:04:25,412 --> 00:04:30,418
when file content is unchanged. One branch
ref is rewritten, once, at the end.

66
00:04:30,458 --> 00:04:34,947
HEAD is detached while the rebase
runs. ORIG_HEAD records the old tip.

67
00:04:34,987 --> 00:04:40,123
The reflogs of HEAD and of the branch,
Git's local lists of where each has been,

68
00:04:40,163 --> 00:04:41,288
record every step.

69
00:04:41,628 --> 00:04:46,654
Now the ID. In one sentence: a commit ID
is a hash of the commit object, the commit

70
00:04:46,694 --> 00:04:51,660
object names its parent and its tree, and
a replayed commit has a different parent

71
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:55,261
and usually a different tree,
so it can't have the same ID.

72
00:04:55,601 --> 00:05:01,063
And because each commit names its parent by
ID, a new ID for one commit forces a new

73
00:05:01,103 --> 00:05:06,302
ID for every commit after it. IDs change
from the first rewritten commit onward,

74
00:05:06,342 --> 00:05:07,350
never before it.

75
00:05:07,690 --> 00:05:12,492
When not to rebase? The textbook calls
it the most important judgment in the

76
00:05:12,532 --> 00:05:17,780
chapter. Both everyday uses are safe on a
branch that only you use, and both become

77
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:21,538
a team incident on a branch
that others have based work on.

78
00:05:23,378 --> 00:05:28,712
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy:
you wrote three amendments to version 4 of

79
00:05:28,752 --> 00:05:32,273
a contract. Meanwhile the
other party issued version 6.

80
00:05:32,313 --> 00:05:37,582
Rebasing is redoing your three amendments,
one after the other, against version 6,

81
00:05:37,622 --> 00:05:41,466
so that the file reads as if
you had started from version 6.

82
00:05:41,806 --> 00:05:46,578
It breaks in two places, and both are the
lesson. First, the three amendments you

83
00:05:46,618 --> 00:05:51,092
wrote against version 4 aren't shredded.
They stay in the drawer, the object

84
00:05:51,132 --> 00:05:55,369
database, reachable through the reflog.
Second, nobody retypes anything.

85
00:05:55,409 --> 00:06:00,062
Each amendment is carried over by a
three-way merge that Git computes, and that

86
00:06:00,102 --> 00:06:02,259
merge can stop and ask you to decide.

87
00:06:02,599 --> 00:06:06,980
From today, stop saying "rebase moves
my commits". Say "rebase copies".

88
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:12,085
The word "moves" hides the originals, and
the originals are where every one of the

89
00:06:12,125 --> 00:06:14,264
CTO's four questions gets answered.

90
00:06:16,104 --> 00:06:21,225
First the before picture, with the real
IDs. feat/rerank has three commits of its

91
00:06:21,265 --> 00:06:23,965
own, cut from 8afc6bd.
main has gained two.

92
00:06:24,305 --> 00:06:28,984
After the rebase, nothing was erased.
Six commits where there were three.

93
00:06:29,024 --> 00:06:34,221
The upper three have no branch, and two
names still reach them: ORIG_HEAD and the

94
00:06:34,261 --> 00:06:36,095
reflog entry feat/rerank@{1}.

95
00:06:37,935 --> 00:06:39,431
Step 1: before.

96
00:06:39,771 --> 00:06:44,347
Three commits on the branch:
5ee19f0, af65a92, bd62876.

97
00:06:44,387 --> 00:06:51,229
Predict: after git rebase main, how many
of these three IDs appear in the branch's

98
00:06:51,269 --> 00:06:53,915
log? Say it out loud. I'll wait.

99
00:06:58,755 --> 00:07:04,018
Rebasing (1/3) is a progress line. In a
terminal the three lines overwrite each

100
00:07:04,058 --> 00:07:07,374
other. The last line names
the one ref that moved.

101
00:07:07,714 --> 00:07:14,487
None of them. The branch now holds 742ab58,
ade2990 and 976a161, on top of 82f1fbb.

102
00:07:14,527 --> 00:07:20,972
Same three subjects, three new IDs. And
the second command shows that the three

103
00:07:21,012 --> 00:07:26,718
original commits still exist: ORIG_HEAD
points at the old tip bd62876.

104
00:07:27,058 --> 00:07:32,425
Step 2: the objects. Put the first commit
of the branch before and after side by

105
00:07:32,465 --> 00:07:37,900
side. Quick quiz before you look. Which
field of the commit object must differ in

106
00:07:37,940 --> 00:07:41,617
the copy? A, the author. B,
the parent. C, the message.

107
00:07:41,657 --> 00:07:42,428
Your answer?

108
00:07:54,268 --> 00:07:59,284
B, the parent, and it's not alone. Three
of the five inputs to the hash changed:

109
00:07:59,324 --> 00:08:04,340
tree, parent, committer. A copy would get
a new ID even with the same parent and

110
00:08:04,380 --> 00:08:09,396
tree, because the committer date moves.
That's why Git doesn't copy commits that

111
00:08:09,436 --> 00:08:14,263
would come out identical. It fast-forwards
over them unless you pass --no-ff.

112
00:08:14,603 --> 00:08:19,596
Step 3: what did not change. A patch ID
is a hash of a commit's diff with line

113
00:08:19,636 --> 00:08:20,628
numbers ignored.

114
00:08:20,968 --> 00:08:25,036
Same patch ID in the first column,
different commit IDs in the second.

115
00:08:25,076 --> 00:08:29,379
Git uses this equivalence to recognise
"the same change under another ID".

116
00:08:29,419 --> 00:08:32,959
You'll meet it again when a
rebase drops a commit on purpose.

117
00:08:32,999 --> 00:08:37,830
And that's how the CTO's fourth question
can be a yes: step one leaves out a commit

118
00:08:37,870 --> 00:08:41,645
whose change is already in the
upstream, and no error is printed.

119
00:08:41,985 --> 00:08:43,630
Step 4: the reflogs.

120
00:08:43,970 --> 00:08:48,963
Read the HEAD reflog from the bottom.
Start, which is a checkout of main.

121
00:08:49,003 --> 00:08:54,617
Three picks. Finish, returning to the
branch. That's the four steps of the manual,

122
00:08:54,657 --> 00:08:59,582
recorded. The branch's own reflog has
one line for the whole rebase, and

123
00:08:59,622 --> 00:09:02,340
feat/rerank@{1} is the old tip, bd62876.

124
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:04,324
Step 5: nothing to do.

125
00:09:04,664 --> 00:09:09,838
Rebase again, and the branch "is up to
date". And rebasing main onto the feature

126
00:09:09,878 --> 00:09:14,986
branch copies nothing: main has no commits
that the branch lacks, so it's moved

127
00:09:15,026 --> 00:09:17,331
forward to the same commit, 976a161.

128
00:09:17,671 --> 00:09:22,154
Step 6: the same rebase by hand.
labs/run ch09/rebase-by-hand.

129
00:09:22,194 --> 00:09:27,551
Detach, cherry-pick the range, move
the branch. git cherry-pick 🟡 CAUTION.

130
00:09:27,891 --> 00:09:32,837
Three new commits on a detached HEAD. Try
it now, thirty seconds, on paper: draw the

131
00:09:32,877 --> 00:09:37,408
graph as it is at this moment, and put
the label feat/rerank where it points.

132
00:09:37,448 --> 00:09:38,654
Then say it out loud.

133
00:09:49,294 --> 00:09:54,289
Still at the old commits. The three new
commits belong to no branch, and only HEAD

134
00:09:54,329 --> 00:09:59,262
reaches them. This is the state a real
rebase is in for most of its running time.

135
00:09:59,602 --> 00:10:04,810
git switch -C <branch> 🟡, git checkout
-B in the manual and in older scripts,

136
00:10:04,850 --> 00:10:09,104
creates the branch at HEAD or,
if it exists, resets it to HEAD.

137
00:10:09,144 --> 00:10:11,353
That's the whole trick of step 4.

138
00:10:11,693 --> 00:10:16,778
Step 7: compare with the real thing. The
demo keeps the hand-made result under a

139
00:10:16,818 --> 00:10:22,095
tag, puts the branch back with git reset
--hard 🔴 on a clean tree, with the old tip

140
00:10:22,135 --> 00:10:25,554
taken from the reflog, and
lets git rebase do the job.

141
00:10:25,894 --> 00:10:31,795
Commit for commit, the trees are identical:
273096f, 9b070ab, 8cc3ece in both lists.

142
00:10:31,835 --> 00:10:36,746
git rebase and three hand-typed
cherry-picks built the same snapshots.

143
00:10:36,786 --> 00:10:42,546
The commit IDs differ only because the real
rebase ran a few lab minutes later, so

144
00:10:42,586 --> 00:10:47,992
its committer dates differ. A commit ID
doesn't record which command made the

145
00:10:48,032 --> 00:10:53,368
commit. It's a function of content:
tree, parents, author, committer, dates,

146
00:10:53,408 --> 00:10:53,934
message.

147
00:10:55,773 --> 00:10:57,422
Five mistakes to watch for.

148
00:10:57,762 --> 00:11:02,557
Saying "rebase moved my commits" and then
looking for the old IDs on the branch.

149
00:11:02,597 --> 00:11:07,452
Root cause: a commit object is never
modified; rebase wrote new objects and moved

150
00:11:07,492 --> 00:11:07,935
one ref.

151
00:11:08,275 --> 00:11:11,462
Expecting the same ID because
the diff is identical.

152
00:11:11,502 --> 00:11:16,425
Root cause: the ID hashes the commit
object, whose parent, committer and usually

153
00:11:16,465 --> 00:11:19,589
tree changed; the patch ID
is what stayed the same.

154
00:11:19,929 --> 00:11:25,285
Believing the originals are deleted. Root
cause: nothing was removed from the object

155
00:11:25,325 --> 00:11:29,589
database; ORIG_HEAD and the branch's
reflog still name the old tip.

156
00:11:29,929 --> 00:11:34,536
Thinking the branch "remembers" where it
started. Root cause: Git has no notion of a

157
00:11:34,576 --> 00:11:39,018
parent branch; the commits to replay are
computed by reachability from the graph.

158
00:11:39,358 --> 00:11:44,547
Rebasing and assuming everything that
recorded an old ID still points at your work.

159
00:11:44,587 --> 00:11:49,462
Root cause: review comments, CI statuses,
deployment records and tags refer to

160
00:11:49,502 --> 00:11:53,117
commit IDs, and those commits
are no longer on the branch.

161
00:11:54,957 --> 00:11:59,911
Now, out of the lab. A training job writes
the commit ID of the code it ran into its

162
00:11:59,951 --> 00:12:04,906
run metadata. Two days later the engineer
rebases the experiment branch, to bring it

163
00:12:04,946 --> 00:12:09,484
up to date before review. The model card
now cites a commit that isn't on any

164
00:12:09,524 --> 00:12:14,360
branch. The commit still exists for a while,
but nobody looking at the branch will

165
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:14,836
find it.

166
00:12:15,176 --> 00:12:20,349
The textbook's rule: before you rebase,
ask what has already recorded these IDs.

167
00:12:20,389 --> 00:12:25,694
A review comment anchored to a commit, a
CI status, a deployment record, the ID in

168
00:12:25,734 --> 00:12:30,842
run metadata. Tags don't follow a rebase.
And signatures are part of the commit

169
00:12:30,882 --> 00:12:35,665
object: a replayed commit is a new
object, signed only if you sign it now.

170
00:12:36,005 --> 00:12:40,320
The "Rebase and merge" button performs
this operation on GitHub's servers.

171
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:45,263
According to the documentation, the commits
that land on the base branch always have

172
00:12:45,303 --> 00:12:50,148
new commit IDs and an updated committer,
and they're unsigned, because GitHub can't

173
00:12:50,188 --> 00:12:55,091
sign on your behalf. This is described from
the documentation, and Chapter 17 covers

174
00:12:55,131 --> 00:12:56,503
the three merge buttons.

175
00:12:58,343 --> 00:13:05,423
Your turn. Do Exercise 9.1, Level 1,
"a plain rebase, before and after", in

176
00:13:05,463 --> 00:13:08,556
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

177
00:13:08,896 --> 00:13:10,863
Predict in writing before the rebase:

178
00:13:11,203 --> 00:13:15,400
Which commits will be replayed? Write
the command whose output is that list.

179
00:13:15,740 --> 00:13:19,641
After the rebase, which names will
still reach the original commits?

180
00:13:19,981 --> 00:13:24,590
For the first replayed commit: which fields
of the commit object will differ from

181
00:13:24,630 --> 00:13:26,484
the original, and which will not?

182
00:13:26,824 --> 00:13:32,170
Then verify each prediction with a command.
The challenge is Exercise 9.4, Level 2,

183
00:13:32,210 --> 00:13:35,609
"the old commits and the new
ones", in the same file.

184
00:13:37,449 --> 00:13:43,028
Q143: "Why does a rebase change commit
hashes: why does every rebased commit have a

185
00:13:43,068 --> 00:13:48,579
new ID even when its diff is identical?
Which fields of the commit object changed,

186
00:13:48,619 --> 00:13:49,866
and which did not?"

187
00:13:52,206 --> 00:13:56,901
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
starts from what a commit ID is computed

188
00:13:56,941 --> 00:14:01,935
from, and goes through the fields one by
one, saying for each whether it changes and

189
00:14:01,975 --> 00:14:06,730
why. It explains why the change propagates
to every later commit and never to an

190
00:14:06,770 --> 00:14:11,525
earlier one. It names the thing that does
stay equal between an original and its

191
00:14:11,565 --> 00:14:16,379
copy, and what Git uses it for. If you can
add what follows for anything that has

192
00:14:16,419 --> 00:14:20,275
stored the old IDs, you have
connected the mechanism to practice.

193
00:14:22,115 --> 00:14:25,042
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

194
00:14:25,382 --> 00:14:30,150
A rebase lists the commits my branch has
and the upstream lacks, detaches HEAD at

195
00:14:30,190 --> 00:14:35,136
the upstream, replays each commit as a
cherry-pick, and then moves the branch to the

196
00:14:35,176 --> 00:14:35,730
last copy.

197
00:14:36,070 --> 00:14:40,720
The copies are new objects: parent,
committer and usually tree differ, so the ID

198
00:14:40,760 --> 00:14:45,645
differs, and every later commit changes with
it. Author, author date and message are

199
00:14:45,685 --> 00:14:47,697
kept, and the patch ID is the same.

200
00:14:48,037 --> 00:14:52,716
The originals are still in the object
database, reachable through ORIG_HEAD and the

201
00:14:52,756 --> 00:14:53,114
reflog.

202
00:14:53,454 --> 00:14:58,270
I can do the same by hand with a detached
HEAD, git cherry-pick of the range, and

203
00:14:58,310 --> 00:14:59,110
git switch -C.

204
00:15:00,950 --> 00:15:04,577
Read sections 9.1 to 9.3 of Chapter 9.

205
00:15:04,917 --> 00:15:10,122
You rebuilt a rebase by hand today, so
there's no magic left in it: it copies, and

206
00:15:10,162 --> 00:15:14,792
then it moves one ref. Do the exercise,
and write your predictions first.

